---
import Slide from "../../components/Slide.astro"
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro"
import { Image } from 'astro:assets'
import prefab_var from "../../img/unity/prefab-var.png"
import tag_menu from "../../img/unity/tag-menu.png"
---
<Slide t1="Spawning Objects">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">We spawn instances of objects from <em>prefabs</em></li>
        <li class="fragment">Create a separate invisible object to do the spawning</li>
        <li class="fragment">Periodic spawning: use of <code>InvokeRepeating()</code></li>
        <li class="fragment">Use of random tagging to vary the instances</li>
        <li class="fragment">Think about cleaning up as well as creating</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Prefabs">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragment">Prefabs are template objects that we can duplicate into the scene</li>
        <li class="fragment">Create a prefab by dragging an object into the <code>Prefabs</code> folder</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Setup">
    <ul>
        <li>The spawning object should have the prefab as a public member variable</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ prefab_var }
            alt="Prefab: Variables"
            height="300"
            width="500" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Instantiation">
    <ul>
        <li>Create instances in the scene with <code>Instantiate()</code></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="150px">
            { `
Vector3 spawnPos = new Vector3(...);
GameObject a = cubePrefab;

GameObject newObj = Instantiate(a, spawnPos, a.transform.rotation);
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li><span class="text-red-500">Caution</span>: don't do this every frame!</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Periodic Spawning">
    <ul>
        <li>We can use <code>InvokeRepeating()</code> to perform an action at a regular
            period (much less than once per frame)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="250px">
            { `
void Start()
{
    InvokeRepeating(nameof(SpawnRandomCube), 2, 1.5f);
}

void SpawnRandomCube()
{
    ...
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Clean-up">
    <ul>
        <li>If game objects are being created continuously, we need to remove them too!</li>
        <li>One approach: each object destroys itself after a certain period of time</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="250px">
            { `
void Update()
{
    _elapsedTime += Time.deltaTime;

    if (_elapsedTime > 10.0) {
        Destroy(gameObject);
    }
}
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Tagging">
    <ul class="text-sm-1">
        <li>We can add tags to objects from the Unity interface</li>
        <li>These tags are project-wide</li>
        <li>The tags need to be created before they can be accessed in code</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <Image
            src={ tag_menu }
            alt="Unity Tag Menu"
            height="300"
            width="400" />
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Working with Tags">
    <ul>
        <li>Set tags on game objects (e.g. when spawning) via the <code>tag</code> property</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="100px">
            { `
gameObject.tag = "A";
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>

    <ul>
        <li>Check for tag presence with <code>CompareTag()</code></li>
    </ul>

    <div class="text-sm-1">
        <CodeBlock lang="csharp" height="100px">
            { `
if (CompareTag("A")) { ... }
            `.trim() }
        </CodeBlock>
    </div>
</Slide>

<Slide t2="Suggestions">
    <ul>
        <li class="fragmnt">Use tagging to spawn objects, randomly choosing between black and white colouring</li>
        <li class="fragment">Use <code>FAST</code> and <code>SLOW</code> tags to create objects moving at
            two different speeds (coin toss)</li>
        <li class="fragment">Use <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code> and <code>Z</code> tags to create objects moving
            back and forth in one of the three axis dimensions (equal chance)</li>
        <li class="fragment">(Do we need to use tags? Can we do the above without them?)</li>
    </ul>
</Slide>
